{"kind": "tool_approval", "tool": "book_chapter_save_draft", "args": {"body": "The air in the Great Library of Aethelgard did not merely smell of old parchment; it smelled of dying history. Ash, fine and grey as ghost-dust, drifted through the shafts of moonlight that pierced the shattered skylights. 

Kaelen pressed his back against a fallen marble plinth, his breath coming in shallow, jagged hitches. Every crackle of the encroaching flames sounded like a bone snapping in the silence of the hall. He couldn't look away from the pedestal at the center of the rotunda. There, bathed in a flickering, unnatural amber light, lay the Ember Codex. It pulsed with a rhythmic, low heat, as if the leather binding were a lung drawing in the smoke.

He had spent three years chasing whispers of this book, and now, with the city in flames and the Inquisition at his heels, the weight of it felt less like a prize and more like a sentence. He reached out, his fingers trembling, the heat from the book warming his skin before he even made contact.", "book_id": "01a02a80-40d5-7434-b136-d0e3aa7b88b1", "chapter": "1", "chapter_id": "01a02a80-4200-7ea2-b3ae-bb3f71cb41e3"}, "tier": "A"}

